 ## Overview
-This method projects a 3D Gaussian primitive, defined by its mean and covariance in world space, onto the 2D image plane to produce a screen-space Gaussian ellipse (splat) for rasterization. It solves the problem of rendering anisotropic, perspective-distorted 3D distributions efficiently by approximating the non-linear perspective projection with a local affine transformation, thereby preserving the Gaussian form in 2D [S1, S3]. Unlike [Gaussian Splatting](946-gaussian-splatting.md) which defines the primitives, this method handles the geometric transformation; it differs from [2D Gaussian Splatting](1456-2d-gaussian-splatting.md) by operating on 3D primitives projected to 2D, rather than native 2D primitives [S3].
+This method projects a 3D Gaussian primitive, defined by its mean and covariance in world space, onto the 2D image plane to produce a screen-space Gaussian ellipse (splat) for rasterization [S3]. It solves the problem of rendering anisotropic, perspective-distorted 3D distributions efficiently by approximating the non-linear perspective projection with a local affine transformation, thereby preserving the Gaussian form in 2D [S1, unofficial, S3].
 
