+- **Limitations:** The linearization error increases for large splats or extreme perspective, potentially causing geometric inaccuracies [S3]. Singularities are handled by stabilization but may introduce slight blurring if $\epsilon_{2d}$ is too large [S2, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from Elliptical Weighted Average (EWA) splatting, described by Zwicker et al. in 2002 for anti-aliased volume rendering [S3]. It was adapted by Kerbl et al. (2023) for 3D Gaussian Splatting, where it projects 3D Gaussians to 2D ellipses for real-time rasterization [S3]. The stabilization term $\epsilon_{2d}$ is a practical addition to ensure numerical robustness in the inverse covariance computation [S2].
